$DIR/main.p:13:7: int64(g()) is a conversion, not a call of a function; or tests the error that a function returns
    An or clause tests the error that a function returns as its last result. A
    conversion, such as int64(x), and a call of a built-in function, such as
    len(s), are written like calls, but they call no function and return no
    error, so there is nothing for or to test.
    Remove the or clause, or call a function that returns an error.
    (see "Or clauses" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.27)
    (run "pluto explain E2461" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:14:7: len(s) is a call of the built-in function len, which returns no error for or to test
    (explained above; run "pluto explain E2461" for more)

$DIR/main.p:15:7: the last result of strconv.Itoa(5) has type string, not error; or tests a last result of type error
    The call before or must return a result of type error last, and the type
    must be error itself. A function that returns no error, or no result at all,
    cannot fail in a way that or can see. A type that implements error, a
    defined type based on error, or a type parameter is not accepted either.
    Remove the or clause, or change the function so that its last result has
    type error.
    With the type exactly error, the test is always the nil test of one
    interface type, and err in the clause is the result itself, not a conversion
    of it.
    Return the error as an error:
        func parse(s string) (int, error)
    (see "Or clauses" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.27)
    (run "pluto explain E2462" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:16:14: the or clause can complete: it must leave with return, break, continue, goto or panic, so that the code after it does not run when f() fails
    The clause of an or statement runs when the call failed, and the code after
    the statement uses the values the call did not produce. So the clause must
    leave: it must be a return, break, continue or goto statement, a call of
    panic, or a block that ends in one of these. An if statement in the block
    leaves only when it has an else branch and both branches leave. A call of a
    function that never returns, such as os.Exit or log.Fatal, does not count,
    since whether a function compiles must not depend on the body of another.
    End the clause with a return, break, continue, goto or panic.
    The or clause is the short form of an if statement that tests an error and
    leaves. Requiring the exit on the line keeps the code after the statement
    from running with values that were never set.
    End the clause with a statement that leaves:
        cfg := load(path) or {
            log.Printf("loading: %v", err)
            return err
        }
    (see "Or clauses" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.27)
    (run "pluto explain E2463" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:17:14: the or clause can complete: it must leave with return, break, continue, goto or panic, so that the code after it does not run when f() fails
    (explained above; run "pluto explain E2463" for more)

$DIR/main.p:18:2: assignment mismatch: 2 variables but strconv.Atoi(s) returns 1 value besides the error
    In an or statement, the values of the call are its results other than the
    last, which is the error that the clause tests. The left side needs one
    operand for each value, and err, which the clause declares, is not one of
    them.
    (see "Assignment statements" in the Plutonium spec, section 12.7)
    (run "pluto explain E0017" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:19:29: an or statement cannot be the post statement of a for statement; test the error in the loop body
    The post statement of a for statement runs at the end of each iteration. An
    or statement cannot be the post statement, since a break or continue in its
    clause would end or repeat the iteration that is ending.
    Test the error at the end of the loop body instead.
    Test the error in the body:
        for i := 0; i < n; {
            ...
            i = next(i) or break
        }
    (see "Or clauses" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.27)
    (run "pluto explain E2465" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:26:14: count has no error result to carry err: its last result has type int, and return with no operands would drop the error
    An unbraced return with no operands in an or clause returns err as the
    function's last result. This function has no result of type error last, so
    there is nowhere to put err, and returning without it would drop the error.
    Handle the error in a block clause, or return values that this function has.
    The short form of return exists to pass an error up. In a function that
    cannot return one, the program must say what to do with the error.
    Handle the error in a block clause:
        load(path) or {
            log.Printf("loading: %v", err)
            return
        }
    (see "Or clauses" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.27)
    (run "pluto explain E2464" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:31:9: main has no results, so no error result to carry err: return with no operands would drop the error
    (explained above; run "pluto explain E2464" for more)
